# EcoSuit MK.I — Dielectric Elastomer Actuators (DEA)

## What they are
A soft capacitor that moves. Two compliant electrodes on a thin elastomer.
Voltage → Maxwell stress squeezes thickness → incompressible rubber expands in plane.
Packaging (stacks, rolls, DEMES) is how you turn that squeeze into a useful stroke.

p = ε E² = ε (V / t)²
et ≈ −ε E² / Y
Figure of merit: ε / Y. Limiters: breakdown, electromechanical snap-through, electrode failure.

## Published performance band
- Useful device strain: 10–50%
- Coupon acrylic area strain: 100–380% with pre-stretch
- Stress: ~100 kPa typical silicone; higher for pre-stretched acrylic
- Energy density: ~0.01–3 MJ/m³ (chemistry-dependent)
- Fields: ~70–400 V/µm → kilovolts unless films are tens of microns
- Commercial stacked SDEA example: ~30 N isometric at ~1.2 kV; ~1.6 mm in tens of ms
- Lab stacks: 100 layers published; 1000-layer lab articles exist

## Materials
| Family | Strength | Space problem |
|---|---|---|
| Acrylic (VHB) | Huge strain after pre-stretch | Creep, humidity, outgas, cold, slow |
| Silicone (PDMS / Elastosil / Sylgard) | Faster, wider T, less creep | Lower raw strain; still an elastomer to qualify |
| PU / filled composites | Tunable ε and modulus | Fillers raise stiffness or make leak paths |

Flight-adjacent work uses silicone, not VHB.

## Electrodes
Must stretch, conduct, and not seed punctures.
- Lab: carbon grease — reject for vacuum / suit
- Flight-ish: carbon black, CNT mats, conductive silicone
- CNT self-clearing: pinhole flash isolates the fault
- Failure: high-frequency bipolar drive can shake CNTs off silicone
- Process: encapsulation + adaptive pre-clearing after build

## Architectures
- Planar membrane — needs a frame if pre-stretched
- DEMES — film on a flex frame; voltage flattens a curl (hatch petal / visor lid in gas)
- Rolled — linear muscle
- Multilayer stack / SDEA — only honest force format; yield is the product
- HASEL — Maxwell stress + liquid pouch. Do not put the liquid in the EcoSuit pressure loop.

DEAs work in air. That is the largest difference from V-EBMs.

## Space evidence (real, not brochure)
- ESA silicone EAP pads on a thruster-pointing demo: TVAC changed stroke via outgassing/moisture (partial recover); ~1.1 kGy Co-60 did not statistically kill stroke
- Stratospheric balloon silicone DEA grippers: 23.6 km, ~0.05 atm, −55 °C
- Carbon-grease electrodes already abandoned in the better vacuum papers
- Not real: flight-qualified stacked DEA on a spacesuit gait joint

## Failure modes
- Weibull breakdown (one defect in a large area wins)
- Electromechanical instability / pull-in
- Electrode delamination or CNT loss
- Leakage → heat → more leakage
- Acrylic viscoelastic drift
- Moisture / outgassing changing t and ε
- UV on exposed organics

## Comparison

| | DEA | V-EBM | Rotary / cable SEA |
|---|---|---|---|
| Cabin air | Yes | No | Yes |
| Hard vacuum | Maybe (silicone, dry electrodes) | Yes | Yes, with vac lube |
| Strain | Large | ~40% of a short cell | Gear / cable geometry |
| Force per cell | Tens of N in stacks | ~4 N @ 6 kV, 0.7 g | Joint-scale N·m |
| Drive | ~0.8–6 kV | 4–6 kV | Low-voltage bus |
| EcoSuit home | Inside, soft goods | Outside, plates | Frame, gait / tools |

## EcoSuit assignment
USE
- Glove / finger assist under the bladder (stacked or rolled silicone, CNT electrodes)
- Wrist / elbow padding assist (DEMES or thin stack)
- Interior hatch petal, LCVG haptics, IVA covers

DO NOT USE
- Hip or knee as primary walk muscle
- Acrylic VHB in vacuum, cold, or on crew
- Carbon grease
- Uninhibited HV in a wet glove
- Exterior plate latches (that is V-EBM territory)

## MK.I coupon
One finger of a pressurized glove: 20–50 layer silicone stack, CNT electrodes, encapsulation, pre-cleared, leak-checked, then thermal-vac as a glove.
If it lives, DEAs earn a slot. If it punches through on cycle 800, they stay in the lab.

## Split
SEAs + cables move the skeleton.
V-EBMs move the ceramic plates in vacuum.
Silicone DEAs move flesh-adjacent soft goods in gas.